- The owner decided to retire the Firefox kiosk launch of Zuzz and use **Brave with bookmarks** for streaming instead, adding a bookmark per service and relying on each site's own fullscreen viewing mode. Firefox stays in the dock as a fallback. This follows from Spec 016 removing the constraint that forced Firefox in the first place, and from the owner running Bitwarden in Brave.
- The change is largely subtractive and consistent with the direction Spec 015 set: it removes the last piece of bespoke session orchestration, replacing a single-purpose managed browser session with an ordinary desktop application and bookmarks.
- Artifacts it would retire or rework: `scripts/launch-zuzz.sh`, `config/systemd/user/zuzz-media.service`, `config/applications/zuzz.desktop`, `scripts/install-browser-media.sh`, and `docs/browser-media.md`.
- **Blocking consequence identified before any work started: the PS button return-home would break.** `scripts/return-home.sh` stops `zuzz-media.service`. A browser launched from a dock entry is not a systemd service; it runs in a transient `app-gnome-brave-NNNN.scope` under `app.slice`. With no `zuzz-media.service` to stop, the PS button would silently do nothing and leave the browser on screen, regressing a Spec 015 acceptance criterion the owner has already tested and accepted.
- `return-home.sh` therefore needs to close foreground application scopes rather than a fixed unit list. That is a real design change and is the main body of work in this switch, not an afterthought.
- Recorded as an owner decision requiring its own specification per the constitution. Nothing has been implemented and Spec 016 is unaffected; the Firefox Zuzz launcher remains in place and working until the replacement is specified, built, and accepted.

## 2026-09-05 — Spec 016 T009 Accepted; Password Manager Raises a Browser Question

- The owner confirmed close-and-relaunch persistence works. T009 is accepted, which retires the last of Spec 009's deferred acceptance — that had stalled purely because there was no way to type a password from the couch.
- The owner also noted they run **Bitwarden in Brave**, which will remove most of the need to type passwords at all.
- **This surfaces a decision worth making deliberately rather than by accident.** Bitwarden lives in Brave, but Zuzz runs in Firefox under `zuzz-media.service`. As configured, the password manager and the media browser are different browsers, so Bitwarden does not help for Zuzz logins.
- The relevant context is that the Brave-to-Firefox switch in Spec 009 was **forced** by Chromium's Wayland text-input behaviour defeating GNOME's on-screen keyboard. Spec 016 removed that constraint entirely: remote input injects at the input layer, so the browser no longer has to cooperate with the compositor. The reason Firefox was chosen no longer exists.
- Recorded as an open question for the owner rather than acted on. Moving Zuzz back to Brave would put streaming logins in the same browser as the password manager, but it is a real change with real work — the launcher, unit, profile handling and the snap-versus-deb profile-path difference that caused the original AppArmor failure — and it would need its own specification per the constitution. It should not be done as a side effect of this feature.
- No change made. Firefox remains the configured Zuzz browser.

## 2026-09-05 — Spec 016 T007/T008 Accepted: Remote Input Works, Including in Chromium

- The owner confirmed phone-based pointer and keyboard input works from the couch with no physical keyboard or mouse.
- Corroborated from the journal that the Chromium half was genuinely exercised: `app-gnome-brave` appears twice for this boot, launched from the dock entry restored earlier, alongside `app-gnome-zuzz-`. Brave was still running at the time of checking. The GSConnect session to the tablet at `192.168.1.210:1716` remained established throughout, and the plugin policy is still intact with `discoverable` false.
- **This closes the problem Spec 009 could not solve.** That specification burned substantial effort on GNOME's on-screen keyboard refusing to appear in Brave, captured a `WAYLAND_DEBUG` trace proving Brave sent a protocol-correct `text-input-v3` handshake that GNOME Shell ignored, and ultimately changed browsers to work around it. Remote input sidesteps the entire class of problem: GSConnect injects events at the input layer, the same way a physical keyboard does, so it does not matter whether the focused application cooperates with the compositor's text-input protocol.
- A consequence worth recording: **browser choice is no longer constrained by keyboard support.** The Spec 009 switch from Brave to Firefox was forced by a limitation that no longer binds. Firefox remains the configured Zuzz browser and there is no reason to change it now, but if that decision is ever revisited it should be on its own merits rather than on keyboard behaviour.
