## 2026-09-05 — Spec 016: GSConnect Installed; Plan Corrected After Reading the Schema

- T002/T003 installed `gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect` `71-1ubuntu1` from `resolute/universe`. The installer is idempotent; the second run reported already-installed. It correctly detected that a newly installed extension is invisible to a running Wayland GNOME session and stopped with instructions rather than failing oddly. The extension is **not yet enabled**; that needs a log out and back in.
- **The plan's central ordering claim was wrong and has been corrected.** It said the plugin policy would be applied before pairing. Reading the shipped schema showed that is impossible: GSConnect stores plugin state **per device** under `/org/gnome/shell/extensions/gsconnect/device/<id>/`, and no device exists until it is paired. There is no global plugin policy to pre-set.
- **The first `configure-gsconnect.sh` was also wrong for a second reason and was rewritten.** It set an `enabled` key on the per-plugin schemas. Those schemas have no such key; the real control is the Device schema's `disabled-plugins` list. The original would have silently done nothing while reporting success — the same failure shape as the input-remapper and liveness-check defects found earlier today. Reading the schema rather than assuming the API is what caught it.
- Recorded the permissive shipped defaults that make prompt policy application matter: `Share.receive-files`, `SFTP.automount`, `Notification.send-notifications` and `Contacts.contacts-source` are all `true` out of the box. `Clipboard` is the one good default, shipping `false` both directions.
- The rewritten script disables every non-input plugin via `disabled-plugins`, additionally clears each risky plugin's own switches as defence in depth so that accidentally re-enabling one does not immediately start moving data, trims the device menu to `keyboard`, and adds `--harden` to turn off GSConnect's LAN discovery once pairing is done. Discovery hardening matters here specifically because the appliance runs no firewall.
- **Dependency footprint recorded per the constitution's explicit-dependencies principle.** The install pulled in 39 packages including `folks`, `telepathy-glib`, `evolution-data-server` and `python3-nautilus` — contacts and telephony stacks present to serve the very plugins this feature disables. Three Evolution user services now run as a result (`addressbook-factory` 9 MiB, `calendar-factory` 9 MiB, `source-registry` 28 MiB; roughly 46 MiB total). All are `static`, D-Bus activated rather than enabled at boot, and none listens on the network. Not masked, because D-Bus-activated services may be requested by other desktop components; noted as available if the owner wants the memory back.
- Next: enable the extension, which requires a session restart, then pair and immediately apply the policy.

## 2026-09-05 — Spec 016 Started: Plan, Tasks, and Environment Baseline

- Branched `feature/0.016-gsconnect-remote-input` from `main` and wrote `plan.md` and `tasks.md` before touching the appliance. Spec status moved to In progress.
- **T001 baseline captured, recorded rather than assumed as the specification requires:**
  - GNOME Shell **50.1**. The package declares `gnome-shell (>= 46~), gnome-shell (<< 51~)`, so 50.1 is supported but only just; a future GNOME 51 will make this package uninstallable. Recorded as a maintenance risk for Spec 013.
  - `gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect` **71-1ubuntu1** from `resolute/universe`, not installed. No third-party source needed.
  - **No host firewall**: `ufw` reports `Status: inactive` and all iptables policies are `ACCEPT`. The specification's firewall criterion is met by recording this; no ports need opening. GSConnect would need TCP/UDP `1716-1764` if a firewall is ever added.
  - Nothing listening on `1714-1719`, so no conflict with an existing KDE Connect install.
  - Appliance at `192.168.1.204` on `enp3s0`.
  - `gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect-browsers` exists but is deliberately not installed; it is link-sharing browser integration and out of scope.
- **Sequencing decision recorded in the plan: the plugin policy is applied BEFORE pairing.** GSConnect enables a broad default plugin set, several plugins of which move data between phone and appliance. Pairing first and pruning later would open a window in which clipboard contents, notifications, and files sync to a living-room machine. Only `mousepad` is wanted; `clipboard`, `share`, `sms`, `telephony`, `notification`, `sftp`, `runcommand`, `mpris`, `systemvolume`, `battery`, `ping`, and `findmyphone` are disabled, each with a recorded rationale.
- Noted as a known exposure rather than glossed over: with no firewall, GSConnect's listening port is reachable by anything on the LAN. This is acceptable only because pairing requires confirmation on both ends.
- T002 wrote `scripts/install-gsconnect.sh`: idempotent, Ubuntu repository only, refuses to run as root, and handles the Wayland constraint that a newly installed extension is invisible to the running GNOME session until the user logs out and back in.
- Not yet run. Installation, enabling, plugin policy, and pairing remain.
## 2026-09-05 — DualSense Will Not Connect After AV-Rack Move; Status False Positive Fixed

- The owner moved the appliance to the AV rack and reported the controller would not connect. SSH and the desktop are unaffected; the appliance is now on Ethernet (`enp3s0`, `192.168.1.204`) with Wi-Fi down.
- Diagnosis: Bluetooth is healthy — `bluetooth.service` active, `hci0` neither soft nor hard blocked, adapter powered, and the DualSense still **paired**. It is simply not connected. `bluetoothctl connect` fails with `br-connection-create-socket` and `bluetoothd` logs `control_connect_cb() connect ...: Host is down (112)`, meaning the controller is not responding. A 15-second scan did not see it advertising, so it is asleep, out of range, discharged, or has re-paired to a PS5.
- **Found and fixed a false positive in `dualsense-media-mode.sh status` that I introduced earlier today.** The liveness check tested only for the `input-remapper DualSense Wireless Controller forwarded` node. That node **outlives the controller**: it was still present with no controller in the room, so `status` cheerfully reported "ON (live injection confirmed)" while the physical device was absent. This is the same class of defect as the original cached-intent report it replaced — a signal that looks authoritative but does not track reality.
- The check now requires the physical controller device to be present **as well as** the forwarded node, and `status` distinguishes three states: injecting, controller connected but not injecting, and controller not connected. The last prints actionable recovery steps and exits non-zero.
- The recovery hint prints the paired Bluetooth address read from BlueZ at runtime rather than tracked in Git, keeping Bluetooth addresses out of version control per the constitution.
- **Separately flagged, not yet fixed:** `rfkill` reports `phy0: Wireless LAN  Soft blocked: yes`. The appliance is reachable only over Ethernet, so the Wi-Fi fallback the constitution requires is currently unavailable. Left for the owner to decide, since it may have been deliberate when the machine moved to the rack.

